<div class="mw-parser-output"><h1>Houses</h1>
<p>A house of your own in Valhalla: a place to keep your things through any 
storm, to rest, and to show the world.
</p><p><b>Buying a house.</b> Haldor the architect, in the Hall of the Citizens 
in Midgaard's Town Hall, sells them: type <a cmd='#'>list</a> there for the prices. 
You must be level 10 or higher, and you can own one house.
</p>
<ul><li>In Udgaard, on Hearth Lane west of the market: a cottage (one room) or</li></ul>
<pre> a house (two rooms). <a cmd='#'>buy cottage</a>, <a cmd='#'>buy house</a>.
</pre>
<ul><li>In Midgaard: a house (two rooms) or a townhouse (three rooms), on</li></ul>
<pre> Penny Lane, Beech Row off Park Road, or Chestnut Close off Royal 
 Avenue. The finer the street, the dearer the house. Name the street 
 when you buy: <a cmd='#'>buy townhouse on chestnut close</a>.
</pre>
<ul><li>Timber and iron make Udgaard's houses much cheaper: logs you fell</li></ul>
<pre> (<a cmd='#'>Help Fell</a>) and hand to John the Lumberjack, and iron ore you mine 
 (<a cmd='#'>Help Mine</a>) for Sam the blacksmith. 10 logs take 90% off a cottage; 
 20 logs and 5 loads of ore 90% off a house in Udgaard; 5 loads of ore 
 90% off a lock. Each piece takes 
 its share, so every one counts, and what a purchase uses is spent. 
 John and Sam tell you your new prices, and <a cmd='#'>list</a> at the architect 
 shows them too.
</pre>
<p><b>Your door.</b> Your house has a front door like any other: <a cmd='#'>open door</a> 
and <a cmd='#'>close door</a> (on a street with several doors, name the way: <a cmd='#'>open 
north door</a>). A new house is open to anyone: having one is something 
in itself. Buy a lock from the architect (<a cmd='#'>buy lock</a>) to keep others 
out. Inside, the owner can then:
</p>
<ul><li><a cmd='#'>lock door</a> and <a cmd='#'>unlock door</a>: while it is locked, only you, those</li></ul>
<pre> you gave a key and the gods come in. You need not fumble with it: walk 
 in or out, and you unlock the door and lock it again behind you. 
 Anyone else finds it locked, and anyone without a key inside is 
 shown out.
</pre>
<ul><li><a cmd='#'>invite &lt;name&gt;</a> gives a friend a key, <a cmd='#'>uninvite &lt;name&gt;</a> takes it back.</li></ul>
<p><a cmd='#'>house</a> shows whose house you are in, its condition, and whether the 
door is locked; the owner also sees who has a key.
</p><p><b>Furniture.</b> Calvin the carpenter on Colosseum Square in Midgaard 
makes beds and chests and has them carried into your house: <a cmd='#'>list</a> in 
his shop.
</p>
<ul><li>A bed you lie in: <a cmd='#'>enter bed</a>, <a cmd='#'>sleep</a>, <a cmd='#'>leave</a>.</li>
<li>Chests for 10, 15 or 25 things (what is in a bag counts too). For</li></ul>
<pre> half a chest's price he fits it with a lock (<a cmd='#'>buy lock for small 
 chest</a>): then only you can open it, door locked or not. Closing it 
 locks it, and it locks itself when you leave it open and go.
</pre>
<p>What is in the chests is kept through any reboot. Things left lying on 
the floor are not.
</p><p><b>Arranging your house.</b> Drag a piece of furniture into another room 
of your house and walk along with it: <a cmd='#'>drag bed north</a>, <a cmd='#'>drag chest up</a>. 
Make the back room your bedroom, or put a chest upstairs. It is heavy 
work, so it costs endurance. Only the owner moves the furniture, it never 
leaves the house, and it stays where you put it through any reboot.
</p><p><b>A blessed bed.</b> Sister Sigrun, a priestess of Odin in Midgaard's 
cathedral, blesses the bed in your house for an offering of 2 platinum: 
<a cmd='#'>bless bed</a> in the cathedral's nave. She walks to your house, in Udgaard 
or Midgaard, and carves the rune ᚨ into the headboard. The blessing 
lasts: whoever sleeps or rests in the bed gets back health, mana and 
endurance twice as fast.
</p><p><b>Selling.</b> <a cmd='#'>sell house</a> at the architect gives your house back to him, 
for half of what it and its lock cost you; say it twice to seal it. 
Calvin's furniture stays with the house, and whatever is in it comes 
back to you. Then you can buy another.
</p><p>Special houses, made to your own plan, can still be asked of the gods.
</p><p><br />
See Also:
</p>
<pre>&gt; <a cmd='#'>Help Fell</a>
&gt; <a cmd='#'>Help Mine</a>
&gt; <a cmd='#'>Help Cart</a>
</pre>




